 "publicacion": {
  "titulo": "Isaac Newton sold before the crash. Then he bought back in.",
  "descripcion": "In April 1720 Isaac Newton, Master of the Royal Mint, decided the South Sea boom would end badly and sold for a profit of about £20,000. The stock kept climbing. In June he moved nearly all his money back in, almost at the peak. By October the stock was worth less than a quarter of that peak, and he lost the whole profit and a good bit more. He was still rich: about £20,000 by mid-1721, against just over £30,000 before the bubble. Some of his 1720 trades are inferred from incomplete records. Not financial advice. Source: Andrew Odlyzko, Isaac Newton and the perils of the financial South Sea, Physics Today, July 2020.",
